-## Caveats
-
-- This is a paper-licensed reduction, not unreduced native-domain coverage. The
-  solver's real search core is a multicolored binary CSP, although the full
-  HR-LQ matching and preference semantics remain visible and are verified.
-- The prime-field predicate is a succinct representation of a very large
-  ordinary graph. The paper proves Theorem 2 for explicit regular graphs; the
-  succinct formula defines the same finite objects but adds exploitable affine
-  structure not analyzed by the paper.
-- The family becomes easy with code: connected components of the marked
-  exclusions are decisive. That success is the Track-B baseline, not an attack
-  failure concealed as hardness.
-- The 0/200,000 density estimate is under the exact prior a statement-aware
-  solver gets for free—one field label per color. It does not estimate a solver
-  biased toward the marked affine relations.
-- Tested attacks are equal-degree/min-label, forward greedy, 256 uniform
-  restarts, and a zero-anchor marked ansatz. No industrial SAT/CP solver or SDP
-  relaxation was run; the exact connected-components decoder is stronger for
-  this generated promise, but may not model every heuristic.
-- `canonical_key` uses all marked triangle and four-cycle types and is invariant
-  under the tested arbitrary relabelings. It is not a complete colored-graph
-  isomorphism algorithm, so rare non-isomorphic collisions remain possible.
-- The mandatory four-vendor bare and G9 runs remain the blocking item. The
-  current `llm_loop_transcript.jsonl` contains only script-authored API errors.
+Section 3.1, Theorem 1 reduces bounded-occurrence 3-SAT and could support a
+future redesign, but it supplies worst-case hardness only. A new inverse-
+planted SAT distribution would need its own attack and oracle evidence; this
+result does not claim such a distribution is hard.
